[["q-115261" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "827d4a3d-b6ab-53b6-bb12-f25faa0e6566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405040} {:answer/id #uuid "383467c9-40c4-5db5-8d12-a8601454a694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405041} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4d8f11-3750-50df-8e99-85f1b881ad96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has a global minimum at $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405042} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcfcbd6-75df-589e-b6e8-48082215ad4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is concave up in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405043} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd3cc61-fa12-54a5-8dfc-015b9b476459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a linear function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "827d4a3d-b6ab-53b6-bb12-f25faa0e6566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405040} :answer-field/id #uuid "76a1b764-1da7-5a71-9954-47bcc426429c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1aea0f50-2894-540a-8561-3e930ff1c7d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115261" :question/problem "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2},$ which of the following must be true?" :question/worked-solution "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)>f(x),\n$$\n\nthat is, the line $L(x)$ must be above the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$"}] ["q-123316" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d14dbef-9a16-537e-a182-87fd6853e543" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4ad089-c48b-5b61-bd06-e37c3bdf81fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405047} {:answer/id #uuid "3837970f-b226-55d0-bfd5-4b3b0950139c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405048} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b6d4df-995b-525a-8e96-c8f41f169657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405049} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c6b25f-b2f3-51ea-ad65-b065219b7fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405050} {:answer/id #uuid "ff072a9c-439d-522d-836f-50edde1fd11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7b6d4df-995b-525a-8e96-c8f41f169657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405049} :answer-field/id #uuid "b405d2fb-dd7e-50bf-8a2a-780bcd6b3b54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd4fed36-e750-5401-88f4-2ac9e7e598e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-123316" :question/problem "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nIII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence I,  the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence II, the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{n}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence III, the dominant term in the denominator is ${n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{n}-3)}{(1+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-3}{1+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence I diverges."}] ["q-123439" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d61aec87-e699-59eb-980b-a6a6cf0bb0f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405053} {:answer/id #uuid "94ab8694-5f19-5ea3-8d98-092aec26cb4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405054} {:answer/id #uuid "a57cacb1-b76d-579c-8c67-b4527f5232ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{9+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405055} {:answer/id #uuid "1eabb9a5-ecc0-5827-9b8a-fa684cf58678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405056} {:answer/id #uuid "9f675dbc-4798-5a7f-b170-06e1c7c60f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d61aec87-e699-59eb-980b-a6a6cf0bb0f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405053} :answer-field/id #uuid "9492d74f-ea7c-56c6-a9a7-679ef961d276" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f58568c3-ce47-5162-ad81-da8c754e43d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-123439" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({sec}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-123458" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac3d5894-f4c5-5d9e-ab7c-cd4cec7b9228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{3+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405060} {:answer/id #uuid "098ef428-487b-52de-8bb3-157513d5af5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405061} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec24e38-78a7-59a2-bcbd-116c50a31d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405062} {:answer/id #uuid "2fff1e42-09de-569f-8b26-6742481f41f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405063} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a7a56e-be07-55b4-a841-3621b9f27de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fff1e42-09de-569f-8b26-6742481f41f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405063} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aaf3974-45a7-5d36-b332-ebb301287f47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9debd71f-7b2d-5a32-ae10-ca946736e9d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-123458" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi $ ?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta })^{2}+{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )+\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1-{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )[1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-126539" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b21e75e6-c690-5426-9022-4e65c4cd597f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405067} {:answer/id #uuid "858df234-13ff-5820-8cad-fd7b874bd17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405068} {:answer/id #uuid "132a6cae-f594-52cb-8c0a-5014dac8b218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405069} {:answer/id #uuid "289d6efd-e337-519a-9f4f-3e9ef2eb9804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405070} {:answer/id #uuid "5e407d05-c2a1-5b46-ab86-013ef5134295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e407d05-c2a1-5b46-ab86-013ef5134295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405071} :answer-field/id #uuid "18f53d24-ffcb-51fb-8ef8-920f533f7ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9b65d2f-1d10-5cc6-91ba-5ed12d5b8509" :question/math-academy-id "q-126539" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c2/c2718471780b5ea4f4b0298624d14a22efb317ef4e8302b9269eeeec9449ca0d.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-126564" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017f8f24-8529-55cc-a1a8-41dc999d12f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405074} {:answer/id #uuid "71c0e048-b5e5-51cc-8ee4-8a54d22a60bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405075} {:answer/id #uuid "53d65de8-c1f0-596d-875a-4aa5f454bb9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405076} {:answer/id #uuid "0592c862-0b50-54dc-acf8-8691c79146ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405077} {:answer/id #uuid "bea2f9bc-178b-5408-a4cc-86ade476f6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bea2f9bc-178b-5408-a4cc-86ade476f6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405078} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca86920-5215-52e0-b0cb-aabc47b3bb45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88dc642a-08f7-5754-86d1-6d7942af6e8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126564" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5},\\,x+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-126748" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8644454b-ee25-59fd-ae65-9770a0b44442" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Bounded by a Closed Curve Using Green’s Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a141bb-7515-52e5-991a-6d1cce5fd112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405081} {:answer/id #uuid "15639b84-a5cd-5c57-999c-5eaa4da55cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405082} {:answer/id #uuid "153703e1-22d9-5301-b881-b997b52a3787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405083} {:answer/id #uuid "08da98f7-5e83-58f1-9af7-d12e18b8d86a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405084} {:answer/id #uuid "bd69317b-3202-5a07-9b1f-77c79a7cbb53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a141bb-7515-52e5-991a-6d1cce5fd112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405081} :answer-field/id #uuid "d59276d0-6151-587a-b33c-8bc27d781877" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0f4b84a-3d7c-5e64-bd13-2b1a225fb6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126748" :question/problem "![](images/65/6571fcdf94818485c2a39e90e018d46e9c95e1acbe0a8cf2f67c688d160a347c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-161862" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65468f34-bcf3-5955-9e74-4fb491f24440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405087} {:answer/id #uuid "9892cc10-60c7-5c80-98f4-7909081e8163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405088} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d9e810-17c8-5408-893c-be33f40e76dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405089} {:answer/id #uuid "300c7f59-2a34-55a7-a5f5-45e7d85f4562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405090} {:answer/id #uuid "a4000a71-03ec-56e5-9ce3-cd0fb0fbb3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4000a71-03ec-56e5-9ce3-cd0fb0fbb3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405091} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5aa772d-380e-5267-8549-8e7d64f48e80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74d7a79e-d57c-5768-84fc-b3ae4dae67d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161862" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨y,0,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-161863" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d53e4587-1cb7-59c0-a9d4-459320b1acca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128y-32{y}^{3}+8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405093} {:answer/id #uuid "d48ffde0-bb06-51b9-8964-4a8d804edc6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128y-32{y}^{3}-8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405094} {:answer/id #uuid "2762b805-3cfe-50ab-a900-9169143dd0a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16y-4{y}^{3}+4{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405095} {:answer/id #uuid "822e52d9-b937-578a-841d-9d75b101780c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32{y}^{2}+32{x}^{2}-16y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405096} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef2bce9-78b4-5c77-bc90-d466fd566e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}^{3}+4{x}^{3}-16y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d53e4587-1cb7-59c0-a9d4-459320b1acca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128y-32{y}^{3}+8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405093} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8698323-72a0-5edf-895c-233cb6848a85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "87ce70cc-a7a3-5bf0-ac23-45c7dbb8a56d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161863" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,z,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z=4{x}^{2}$ and $z=16-4{y}^{2}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=16-4{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=4{x}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cylinder ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(16-4{y}^{2})=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(16-4{y}^{2})=-8y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=z=16-4{y}^{2},\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 0-(16-4{y}^{2})\\cdot (-8y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(128y-32{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cylinder ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2})=8x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=z=4{x}^{2},\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 8x-4{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-8x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(128y-32{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}8x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}128y-32{y}^{3}+8x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-161896" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "855f9cab-5970-5823-b1ec-e6f8c8330244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405100} {:answer/id #uuid "feb823c9-ae29-56b1-bece-172402691eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405101} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9ee25f-ef8f-511d-86bf-e7005eafe934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405102} {:answer/id #uuid "6bef0962-a68e-52b4-9ae4-4e5699c26ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405103} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9eb018-8e9f-52c9-8219-0eed349fe16b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feb823c9-ae29-56b1-bece-172402691eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405101} :answer-field/id #uuid "1886a1f4-707b-5268-9cc1-ed7effda4966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d7ee053-a1f8-527b-9332-a3761ff9b930" :question/math-academy-id "q-161896" :question/problem "![](images/14/1422e6534c001214335dcddb8888f7e2e9344e440379c47da38514e9a3ef0bd8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,2⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z=1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q=0,\\,R=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x+2\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x+2\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-161897" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b802cb74-ee56-571a-ba2b-74472252f1f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405107} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7781c-10a9-5713-ab76-29cfaffd54d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405108} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6025cd-45c8-596b-b893-c5fd2b0d6043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405109} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f9eadd-2663-5edf-a933-30b97b13faa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405110} {:answer/id #uuid "11453532-2079-5fc7-a118-668744b23d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a6025cd-45c8-596b-b893-c5fd2b0d6043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405109} :answer-field/id #uuid "51599c73-a498-5c6e-99ec-3daf9c417dd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fddd3a8-32de-55ef-b058-e60e47b22c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-161897" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6af3d84594e2b2648dcbbae05a64f03ef5a38c76f5a1ed835fc4c6b6f50ef97a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-x,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-x,\\,R=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(-x)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-x,-0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-161901" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263b1feb-971b-5d7f-8e46-5b3bc0e94dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405114} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4e563f-2961-582e-92cd-4ac00f3541da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405115} {:answer/id #uuid "94cdc7b9-f47b-525c-b5b6-ab08d22fded3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405116} {:answer/id #uuid "34969a97-52a8-5790-8d57-5135e6098783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405117} {:answer/id #uuid "c701af60-222c-5761-9908-aa65b644b188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "263b1feb-971b-5d7f-8e46-5b3bc0e94dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405114} :answer-field/id #uuid "55ff700f-c93e-5ab0-80b1-0ec031f0502f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3eea45a-e598-5192-b757-2e1b9a1153d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161901" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7f140ad2c5687db48171819e1efaa858701af881ebbee57ff835da569f30947.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨{z}^{2},0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-161903" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86b9be73-bab1-55d5-99f8-7629e8fdfac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405121} {:answer/id #uuid "03b65f71-c8ab-568a-a5b4-8a9e1e082302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405122} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ec3f6d-843d-5ada-bec6-0a99ea632881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405123} {:answer/id #uuid "62a9e402-0459-5909-abf2-55ce70fd1f54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y+\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405124} {:answer/id #uuid "c486fa33-cd09-56ea-8bcb-3f7d7b5b1b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7ec3f6d-843d-5ada-bec6-0a99ea632881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405123} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8800b1d-0dbb-5dd9-8914-c007ddb67ebc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8fd3ad8-a86b-542f-ab4a-6226468a4f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161903" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-z,\\,2y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4,\\,R=2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(0)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-z,2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-161904" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e0745c0-c1e3-54ed-89c3-84df7cb535ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405128} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc9ee65-567a-55e7-8e17-a3faf479965a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405129} {:answer/id #uuid "1d66daf3-c66b-5610-a4a1-945ece3d502b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405130} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd338b1-bf95-5ed9-80a5-7996beec69f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405131} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2bc3ec-eeb8-52c4-94c5-3b19fba9b500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e0745c0-c1e3-54ed-89c3-84df7cb535ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405128} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf9dad5-46da-52ad-9f58-b7b22be13881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "150a753b-c416-5943-8cfa-2d89a5b17df4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161904" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨-y,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the hemisphere $z=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=1.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-y,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-(0)\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-161906" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7a7f391-3bf1-5e65-8965-222079e382ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405135} {:answer/id #uuid "df134500-f679-5b88-b68e-c5837a8dbd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405136} {:answer/id #uuid "e34e8100-bd58-5269-b3f2-be790484b855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy+y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405137} {:answer/id #uuid "122a4785-5a24-5fd5-b4c3-e984dd5711a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405138} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9a02b5-0a40-5aa5-afaf-1cec057e3153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d9a02b5-0a40-5aa5-afaf-1cec057e3153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405139} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a4579ec-958a-5fd2-805d-97cd0da00894" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8cd36a0-19ac-53de-ada3-7c525383dea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161906" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,x,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=1-\\frac{1}{2}x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=x,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=x,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-x\\cdot 0+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+y)\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-161908" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d997b13e-de60-5563-841f-2fd5ee29d99e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405142} {:answer/id #uuid "c47bf5a7-ddbb-55ea-94c5-903a6bd874ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405143} {:answer/id #uuid "a933c2f8-9508-5563-b677-d1cf1467dfc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405144} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7cafee-a688-51a7-b3d1-4d1daf466854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405145} {:answer/id #uuid "156642d0-8eb1-5036-896e-6fea2101a8de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e7cafee-a688-51a7-b3d1-4d1daf466854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405145} :answer-field/id #uuid "9854d104-9a4b-53d3-8285-a6f5f2377a4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "883193fd-f5be-5d1c-8ffa-32b09d1431cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161908" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2fff2eab1f5670dfcd3351dcbb9be1b3110e6ebdcd452cf4c5a273611d454c8b.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-161913" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cefe31a-522d-5318-b0c1-4892165c8d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405149} {:answer/id #uuid "6dbfd4cf-3634-5dbd-b50c-a2a6baa15297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405150} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d797f0-c71c-527a-a6dd-aeedb88fb5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405151} {:answer/id #uuid "1a998466-68ff-5ef7-bee3-792b48a2d00f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405152} {:answer/id #uuid "265f91a3-d619-5622-b9e3-15ca49b4b395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "265f91a3-d619-5622-b9e3-15ca49b4b395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405153} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae335d47-3b07-5474-9957-6ab74654f8ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b5db020-d92f-50c2-8c6b-3f44b9d6b975" :question/math-academy-id "q-161913" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the parabolic cylinder $z={y}^{2}-2.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={y}^{2}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(0)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cylinder ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-2)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}={({y}^{2}-2)}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-{({y}^{2}-2)}^{2}\\cdot (0)+0\\cdot (2y)+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-32876" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109c8716-781f-5d87-93da-32c8a82a52af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(\\pi \\,i+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405156} {:answer/id #uuid "2a440298-d0fd-5f7c-ade9-88076046ff22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(\\pi \\,i+\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405157} {:answer/id #uuid "62d667da-2ed7-5305-afe2-d228189c5402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(\\pi \\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405158} {:answer/id #uuid "6b671d99-5618-53cb-8be2-7e346528c73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(-\\pi \\,j-\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405159} {:answer/id #uuid "e549fd1f-32f0-5d47-bc19-3d8ea851a377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(i+\\pi \\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62d667da-2ed7-5305-afe2-d228189c5402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u(\\pi \\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405158} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc1f347e-fe06-5ef7-a082-5d1425a121dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33bfb8ae-51e5-575a-aee2-202f3434e957" :question/math-academy-id "q-32876" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j+t\\,k$ at the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ valid for real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =-i+0\\,j+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j+t\\,k & =-i+0\\,j+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t=-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t=0 \\\\ t=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=\\frac{3}{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{3}{2}).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{3}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+\\pi \\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u\\,(\\pi \\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] ["q-36754" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124556c0-f467-5318-900e-7da585d6554e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405163} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7a1b1e-b535-5117-9c36-e366c14a4377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405164} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1353fa-aa2b-5093-bcea-f8bdbde39ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405165} {:answer/id #uuid "699346b8-439a-5fe0-9c40-fa77c142405e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405166} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce9fec0-c766-50ce-8550-9639ade8ba3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩+t\\,⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a7a1b1e-b535-5117-9c36-e366c14a4377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405164} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0a835b-df77-526f-b20f-c5410f491aab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7c74ce4-3c4e-5ea4-ab88-89558065f279" :question/math-academy-id "q-36754" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ at the point $(0,0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we can write the equation of our surface as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,2) & =2(0)\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{(2)}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the vector form of the equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,0,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,0,2)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] ["q-51252" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9609247-0e03-57ea-b5ae-e4d8317f21cd" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Contextual Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1755998a-36e1-51b0-812d-c46291886319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(t-200),\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405170} {:answer/id #uuid "96e03499-7bbd-5bef-b433-2a1a2207d8b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3kt-200,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405171} {:answer/id #uuid "d4629166-0d65-58a8-9a9a-881e20adbb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt{t}-200,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405172} {:answer/id #uuid "34ef341b-7fa7-5aa2-a8bb-887730adc385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405173} {:answer/id #uuid "9e33bad6-05d7-530a-aa2b-fd679f7dc3b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kt-200,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e33bad6-05d7-530a-aa2b-fd679f7dc3b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kt-200,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405174} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0ed738b-bc03-5b59-b753-afa4be6b0216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc6d7af4-796f-502e-85cf-2c3e6d5e60eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-51252" :question/problem "A tank is being filled with water. The rate of change of the volume of water in the tank $V,$ in milliliters, is directly proportional to the time $t,$ measured in hours. Additionally, the tank is also leaking $200\\,\\text{ml}$ of water every hour. What is the differential equation that describes the rate of change of the volume of water in the tank, at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time (in hours) since the tank started filling?" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the volume of water in the tank per hour increases in proportion to $t$ and decreases by $200.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kt-200,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality."}] ["q-72575" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9415a6bc-b845-5416-a630-c09cf1de4075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405177} {:answer/id #uuid "d774e169-e628-59dd-b3d3-32eef4cc221b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405178} {:answer/id #uuid "820bcbf2-b300-5622-88e4-cea1d1927af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405179} {:answer/id #uuid "6a49ee16-b14b-5b39-9515-f019516811a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405180} {:answer/id #uuid "47b78352-073a-55e3-977a-ffd9d1cb0ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47b78352-073a-55e3-977a-ffd9d1cb0ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405181} :answer-field/id #uuid "cca85be9-de3b-5573-9094-83fec95f1da6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbb2727c-01c4-5345-9e38-1d38c7fb43da" :question/math-academy-id "q-72575" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since ${b}_{22}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is **not** in row echelon form."}] ["q-79044" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "736006b2-c984-5f16-99e0-7bede131ce8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405184} {:answer/id #uuid "bab5e31a-96b2-50df-a788-c26d61f45ce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405185} {:answer/id #uuid "8e106efb-4b05-5489-9405-38ad43685dd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1-\\sqrt{11},0)$ and $(1+\\sqrt{11},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405186} {:answer/id #uuid "77d6e2e0-104a-585f-8b8c-f66417a730a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405187} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa8b8a3-06d9-587f-b744-e844d9ffa161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{11},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "736006b2-c984-5f16-99e0-7bede131ce8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405184} :answer-field/id #uuid "49673678-7bc5-5316-be28-063bdf75dac5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f43a31ca-f582-5ca4-a303-c0af1e9c11d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-79044" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12=0$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2x+6\\cdot 0+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+1+12 & =0+1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+12 & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x-1)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x-1)^{2}=-11.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea1410ea792ac5fb09316bc6d417c2ba46f84c050491b1e351f360eac50deb6c.png)"}] ["q-79315" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78f2fba8-448d-54b0-9f0b-a7a12167cb79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405191} {:answer/id #uuid "81d0803a-89e6-5850-b97e-24717c7d2073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kD,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405192} {:answer/id #uuid "a3657738-9337-52ad-bf3f-63b326fd491f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{kt}{D},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405193} {:answer/id #uuid "60e1f64b-1691-53e6-9e99-dae3ec2ed84b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{t},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405194} {:answer/id #uuid "67a35e15-d65b-53a5-850a-945cbcf431a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{D},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78f2fba8-448d-54b0-9f0b-a7a12167cb79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405191} :answer-field/id #uuid "46d988ad-b19d-531c-8043-d4cc3d8d9aff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ba66d242-26a0-594d-82a7-1f167d592d18" :question/math-academy-id "q-79315" :question/problem "The rate of growth of a car's displacement increases at a rate that's proportional to the time $t$ since the car started moving. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the car's displacement, $D,$ at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The rate of growth of a **car's displacement** $D(t)$ **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **time** $t$ since the car started moving.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *car's displacement* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *interval of time* since the car started moving. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}∝t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the car's displacement *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kt.\n$$"}] ["q-79945" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7aa5350-6bfc-5057-ad4a-3bd97a31da5e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Series Solution to a Second-Order Initial Value Problem About a Non-Zero Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4635909d-d1cb-573a-bd44-d8c587978343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405198} {:answer/id #uuid "1136a0bc-37ae-5098-9fa3-ad53832c8724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405199} {:answer/id #uuid "f096707f-5894-5389-997f-94a4fa989704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405200} {:answer/id #uuid "b86c8e20-b728-52b1-b463-0e009c18ac02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405201} {:answer/id #uuid "2016a2e2-9e4e-5a38-9fa3-a7d1bc597b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2016a2e2-9e4e-5a38-9fa3-a7d1bc597b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405202} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5283d39-dcd8-5df6-aae7-c396d0691e3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2480529-9e51-5e8c-9f4c-f59546697173" :question/math-academy-id "q-79945" :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=\\pi $, in ascending powers of $(x-\\pi )$ up to and including the term in $(x-\\pi )^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(\\pi )=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=\\pi $:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\pi ),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi ).$\n\nWe substitute $y(\\pi )=1$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(\\pi ) & ={y}^{2}(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=2y{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi )$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi ) & =2y(\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+{y}^{2}(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =2(1)(-1)(0)+(1)^{2}(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯ \\\\  & =1+(x-\\pi )\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2}\\cdot (0)+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-1)+… \\\\  & =1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-97990" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a252908-6cba-57b3-9ed0-dd7e95aa69c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405205} {:answer/id #uuid "c85e28c4-0aa8-5374-afe0-82128df5ed7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405206} {:answer/id #uuid "12ee5128-654b-5538-a73f-170cdd7589be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405207} {:answer/id #uuid "5810134f-edb2-5145-b526-fe3657df320f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405208} {:answer/id #uuid "16848eb1-6562-5462-b01d-8bdf402e1e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c85e28c4-0aa8-5374-afe0-82128df5ed7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405206} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d2f6ab-2224-5973-9cb7-233b0a4ecb90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fb95d9e-0ced-5515-baa2-9595c0717556" :question/math-academy-id "q-97990" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=7.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=7\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] ["q-97995" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e022e2a9-7ada-59ab-9b55-0e78c7221355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405212} {:answer/id #uuid "891a2dde-5575-57e6-8a39-eba10064aba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405213} {:answer/id #uuid "36bf5957-c85d-532e-a8e3-8bcd5ef2026b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405214} {:answer/id #uuid "581abcc9-7db9-51b9-92f8-690c96003338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405215} {:answer/id #uuid "a984d186-939c-58fa-963a-f7fbf679d08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "891a2dde-5575-57e6-8a39-eba10064aba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405213} :answer-field/id #uuid "efb02de0-491f-59d6-8a75-57bfea7150b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "091c06c4-0cfd-5952-9df6-eba9b260f93a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97995" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=7.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=7\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}]]
